query:
I eat one or two squares of dark chocolate most evenings. I am trying to work out whether replacing an approximately two-dollar supermarket bar with a ten-to-twelve-dollar craft bean-to-bar habit would improve my experience enough to justify the recurring premium. Those figures are only my budget anchors, not verified current prices.

The frozen shopping set contains five listings: a $25.45 twelve-pack of 3.30-ounce DOVE dark bars; a $30 twelve-pack of 3.5-ounce Lindt 70-percent bars; one $8.99 100-gram Ritter Sport 74-percent bar; a $21.99 three-pack of 100-gram Green & Black 70-percent bars; and a $14.95 three-pack of 1.8-ounce Raaka coconut-milk 60-percent bars carrying bean-to-bar, small-batch, single-origin, organic and direct-source claims. The Raaka title implies 5.4 ounces across three bars, while Product Dimensions reports 1.8 ounces. The background material covers bean-to-bar production control, cocoa-solids terminology, bulk-versus-flavor cocoa classifications, Fairtrade price support, analytical and affective sensory testing, nine-point hedonic liking, blinding, and three limited community anecdotes. It does not contain matched blind-taste results, verified current labels and delivered prices, or a universal price at which quality stops improving.

Please audit the evidence and design a practical way for me to decide how far, if at all, to move up this price ladder. Use these boundaries:

- keep all prices ratings review counts pack sizes masses percentages origin certification process flavor and quality wording at frozen seller assertion scope;
- retain the Raaka three times 1.8 ounce title versus 1.8 ounce Product Dimensions conflict until the delivered offer is verified;
- show price per bar and price per declared mass arithmetic but do not hide pack assumptions or mass conflicts;
- keep the two dollar and ten to twelve dollar figures as unverified budget anchors unless current exact offers match them;
- separate comparable plain dark bars from flavored coconut containing or otherwise unmatched products;
- do not infer quality preference or health benefit from price cocoa percentage rating origin or marketing copy;
- treat bean to bar as process control and Fairtrade as price support context not as taste health traceability payment or total ethics guarantees;
- distinguish dry cocoa solids from broader legal cocoa solids usage and do not infer a complete formula from percentage alone;
- treat bulk versus flavor cocoa as a partly subjective processing sensitive classification not a grade for an exact bar;
- keep each forum statement at its author product incident place and time scope;
- verify current exact SKU variant pack mass price shipping ingredients allergens cocoa components origin certification lot date storage and return terms before purchase or tasting;
- do not make health benefit safe daily intake or medical claims;
- separate detectable difference from hedonic liking and willingness to pay;
- mask brand package price and marketing randomize equal coded portions repeat sessions and record unblinding where practical;
- predeclare the minimum liking gain maximum incremental evening cost repeatability rule and identity allergen package and sensory stops;
- report any price stop only for this buyer these verified bars prices portions and test conditions; and
- choose the lowest cost exact comparable bar that passes identity allergen repeatable sensory and budget gates otherwise keep the current bar run a smaller corrected trial or defer.

Compare these possible approaches rather than assuming one in advance:

1. buy DOVE or Lindt because the lowest normalized seller price must mean the best daily value;
2. buy Ritter or Green & Black because a higher single bar price or cocoa percentage must mean better quality;
3. buy Raaka because bean to bar single origin small batch organic and beyond fair trade copy must guarantee superior taste and ethics;
4. rank all five as one price ladder despite percentage recipe coconut content pack and mass differences;
5. infer the universal quality cutoff from store ratings marketing copy or three community anecdotes;
6. use one unblinded taste after seeing brand and price as proof of a measurable improvement; or
7. choose the lowest cost exact comparable bar that passes identity allergen repeatable sensory and budget gates otherwise keep the current bar run a smaller corrected trial or defer.

Build an exact-offer comparison with literal pack, mass, price, percentage, recipe, process, ethics, rating and unknown fields, and show the unit-price arithmetic without smoothing over the Raaka conflict. Explain what bean-to-bar, Fairtrade, cocoa percentage, cocoa-solids terminology, bean class, ratings and anecdotes can and cannot establish. Then give me the smallest workable repeated within-person test: comparable products and portions, masked identity and price, randomized coded samples, repeated sessions, an unblinding record, and separate measures for detectable difference, nine-point liking and willingness to pay. State the label, allergen and current-offer checks required before tasting, predeclare a person-specific next-tier stopping rule, and finish with a lowest-cost purchase, keep-current-bar, corrected-trial or deferral outcome. Do not claim a universal market cutoff or make a health recommendation.
